# Observability: Operations

Purpose:
  Define operational observability: cost tracking, token efficiency,
  tool/agent/skill performance, budget policy, data classification,
  secret redaction, provenance, root cause analysis, and failure taxonomy.

Owns: architecture/observability/operations.sdd

Principles:
  Observability data MUST be classified (PUBLIC, INTERNAL, CONFIDENTIAL, SECRET).
  Sensitive data MUST be redacted in all observability outputs.
  Budget policy is enforced during execution.
  Failures are categorized, not just logged as errors.
  Alerts are actionable and have owners.
  Observability feedback loop enables self-improvement with governance.

CostTracking:
  metrics:
    input_tokens
    output_tokens
    total_tokens
    estimated_cost

  example:
    EXEC-500:
      input = 120k
      output = 18k
      cost = $0.82

  cost_per_task:
    TASK-AUTH: average_cost = $0.31
    TASK-PAYMENT: average_cost = $2.80

  cost_per_agent:
    AGENT-CODER: $500/month
    AGENT-QA: $180/month
    AGENT-ARCHITECT: $220/month

TokenEfficiency:
  metric:
    task_success / token_usage

  rule: high token usage for simple tasks indicates skill/context problems

ToolPerformance:
  metrics:
    tool_calls_total
    tool_failures
    average_latency
    timeout_rate
    retry_rate

  example:
    go_test: failure_rate = 12%

AgentPerformance:
  metrics:
    success_rate
    failure_rate
    average_steps
    average_tokens
    average_cost
    average_duration
    retry_rate

SkillPerformance:
  metrics:
    success_rate
    average_duration
    failure_rate

  example:
    SKILL-REPOSITORY-PATTERN: success_rate = 96%
    SKILL-LEGACY-MIGRATION: success_rate = 61%

  rule: skill performance drives improvement decisions

ObservabilityFeedbackLoop:
  flow:
    Execution -> Observability -> Metrics -> Analysis -> Skill Improvement -> New Version -> Execution

  rule: system can self-improve based on observability
  rule: auto-changes require governance

DataClassification:
  levels:
    PUBLIC: no restrictions
    INTERNAL: internal use only
    CONFIDENTIAL: restricted access
    SECRET: highly sensitive

  rule: all observability data MUST be classified
  rule: SECRET data is never exposed in logs or traces

SecretRedaction:
  rule: secrets MUST be redacted in all outputs
  example:
    Authorization: Bearer eyJ... -> Authorization: [REDACTED]
    DATABASE_URL=postgres://... -> DATABASE_URL=[REDACTED]

  rule: observability system must not create security problems

BudgetPolicy:
  structure:
    budget:
      max_tokens: 200000
      max_cost: $2
      max_duration: 30m
      max_tool_calls: 100

  rule: budget exhaustion triggers PAUSE or DENY
  rule: budget policy is enforced by policy engine

Provenance:
  definition:
    record of how a result was produced

  example:
    Artifact-90:
      created_by: EXEC-300
      derived_from: TASK-200
      implements: REQ-100

RootCauseAnalysis:
  flow:
    Task failed
      -> trace back through execution
      -> identify root cause
      -> example: root_cause = TOOL_TIMEOUT

FailureTaxonomy:
  categories:
    LLM_FAILURE
    TOOL_FAILURE
    NETWORK_FAILURE
    POLICY_DENIED
    VALIDATION_FAILURE
    TIMEOUT
    CONTEXT_FAILURE
    DEPENDENCY_FAILURE
    USER_INPUT_REQUIRED
    SYSTEM_FAILURE

  rule: failures are categorized, not just logged as errors

Alerting:
  ai_specific_alerts:
    - agent_retry_rate > 5
    - token_usage > expected_budget
    - execution_failure_rate > 20%
    - production_policy_denials > threshold

  rule: alerts are actionable and have owners

Navigation:
  Architecture: @../INDEX.sdd
  System: @../system.sdd
  AI: @../ai.sdd
  Events: @../events.sdd

Rules:
  [OBS-09] Cost and token metrics are required for AI operations.
  [OBS-10] Skill performance metrics drive improvement decisions.
  [OBS-11] Failure taxonomy enables better alerting and analysis.
  [OBS-12] Budget policy is enforced during execution.
  [OBS-15] Agent MUST NOT add signatures or sign-offs to observability outputs.
  [OBS-16] Agent MUST NOT use emojis, icons, or decorative symbols in observability output headers or titles — reserved for human use only.

State: +
